    {
      "path": ".claude/scripts/with-browser-lock.sh",
      "content": "A zero-exit scan containing the wrapper record is still accepted when other records are malformed or the output is partial. The parser silently skips malformed records, so a scan containing the wrapper but a corrupted remembered descendant row reaches the `%known` rebuild, forgets that live descendant, and can report zero members and release the lock. This violates B17's partial/unparseable-output handling. Track parsing failures and reject incomplete scans without rebuilding `%known`; add a ps-stub case that retains the wrapper row while corrupting a remembered descendant row (the current tests cover only nonzero exit and completely empty output).",
      "existing_code": "  my $ok = close($ps) \u0026\u0026 $? == 0;\n  return undef unless $ok \u0026\u0026 exists $p{$$};",
      "start_line": 96,
      "end_line": 97,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "path": ".claude/scripts/with-browser-lock.sh",
      "content": "Use the full identity for signal deduplication as well. The successful-scan branch of `signal_all` still keys `%sent` by PID and `start` only. If a PID is reused within the same second by another actual member (recognized through its token or parent), the new process inherits the previous process's TERM/KILL bookkeeping despite having a different command line. A detached replacement can therefore escape signalling. Key that branch by `id`, consistently with `%known` and the invalid-scan branch, and add a regression case for a replacement that still qualifies as a member.",
      "existing_code": "    $p{$pid} = { pgid =\u003e $pgid, start =\u003e $start, id =\u003e \"$start\\x01$rest\", token =\u003e ($rest =~ /(?:^|\\s)\\QTJ_BROWSER_LOCK_TOKEN=$token\\E(?:\\s|$)/ ? 1 : 0) };",
      "start_line": 93,
      "end_line": 93,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ],
